Final Verdict

The project's core machine learning architecture is sound. The model, dataset preparation, evaluation metrics, risk estimation, and object representation are all modular and aligned with trajectory prediction research. The gap is in the inference orchestration: the current main.py processes each trajectory sample independently, even though the processed dataset retains enough metadata (log_id, timestamp_ns, track_uuid) to reconstruct complete scenes.

Because of that, I would freeze almost the entire codebase and make only targeted changes:

Expose metadata from TrajectoryDataset.
Group samples into scenes during inference.
Write scene-based results.json.
Reuse the existing renderer with the richer JSON.

This is an engineering completion task, not a machine-learning redesign. Once that is in place, you can confidently run the LSTM experiment, validate the pipeline with a short smoke test, and then perform the full training run.
